[Event "Knight School"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit"]
[Variation "Queen's Gambit Declined: Baltic Defense"]
[ECO "D06"]
[Character "positional"]
[Risk "low"]
[EarlyMate "none"]
[Tricks "none"]
[Themes "bishop-pair,centre-pawns"]
[For "white"]
[Kind "sideline"]
[Opponent "2...Bf5"]
[Start "orthodox 2.c4"]

2... Bf5 { Brings the light-squared bishop out before ...e6 can shut it in. It leaves the pawn on d5 and the square b7 with less protection. } 3. cxd5 { Takes the pawn. Black has no pawn to retake with, and the queen or a knight will have to do it. } 3... Bxb1 ( 3... Nf6 4. Nf3 { Develops and guards d4. If Black now takes on b1, Qa4+ wins time against the king. } 4... Bxb1 5. Qa4+ Qd7 6. Qxd7+ Nbxd7 7. Rxb1 { White has the two bishops and Black still has to win back the pawn on d5. } ) ( 3... Qxd5 4. Nc3 { Develops with a hit on the queen. } 4... Qd8 5. e4 { Takes the centre and attacks the bishop, which must move again. } ) 4. Rxb1 { Takes back. White has given up the knight for the bishop and keeps the two bishops for the rest of the game. } 4... Qxd5 5. Nf3 { Develops and guards d4. } 5... Nf6 6. a3 { Moves the pawn off a2, where the queen on d5 could take it, and stops ...Bb4+. } 6... e6 7. g3 { The bishop goes to g2, aiming down the long diagonal at the queen and at b7. } 7... Be7 8. Bg2 O-O 9. O-O { Plan: b4 and Bb2, then e4 to open the centre for the two bishops. Black has no light-squared bishop to fight them. } *
